classTweaker v1 official

# Access control for the 26.x targets. One file, three versions:
#
# Prism's accessWidener(...) in the root build script is the only place this
# is declared; it feeds Fabric as-is and converts the same file into a NeoForge
# access transformer. It is byte-identical across 26.1, 26.2 and 26.3 on
# purpose: PORTING_PLAN.md 10.1 diffs those three trees excluding only
# pack.mcmeta, so a version-specific word in a comment here fails the gate.
# The one header difference, 1.21.11 named vs 26.x official, is on line 1 and
# that file is excluded from the 1.21.11 comparison.
#
# The header above is the entire 1.21.11 -> 26.x difference. A 1.21.11 target is
# obfuscated and remapped by fabric-loom-remap, so it needs the "named"
# namespace. A 26.x target ships unobfuscated, where Loom expects "official" --
# which on 26.x is already the Mojang name, so the NeoForge conversion stays
# correct on both.
#
# Two format rules, both learned the hard way:
#
#   1. The header must be line 1. Loom's ClassTweakerReaderImpl.readHeader
#      reads the first line verbatim and does not skip comments, so a file that
#      opens with a comment fails with "line 1: Invalid access widener file
#      header" even though the header is present further down. Prism's own
#      access transformer converter tolerates it, so the error only appears on
#      the Loom side and looks like a corrupt file.
#   2. Separators are SPACES. A tab-separated header is rejected the same way.
#      Same split personality: Prism converts it, Loom refuses it.

# --- Phase 2: block entity types -------------------------------------------
# 26.1 deleted BlockEntityType.Builder, and 26.2 then deleted the static
# register(String, BlockEntitySupplier, Block...) that had replaced it --
# 26.2's BlockEntityType.java contains zero occurrences of "register". The two
# construction routes are not interchangeable and neither survives every target,
# so the constructor is what gets widened instead:
#
#   private BlockEntityType(BlockEntitySupplier<? extends T>, Set<Block>)   // 26.1
#   public  BlockEntityType(BlockEntitySupplier<? extends T>, Set<Block>)   // 26.2+
#
# Verified on 26.1 with javap -p, quoted verbatim from the merged vanilla jar:
#
#   private net.minecraft.world.level.block.entity.BlockEntityType(
#       net.minecraft.world.level.block.entity.BlockEntityType$BlockEntitySupplier<? extends T>,
#       java.util.Set<net.minecraft.world.level.block.Block>);
#
# Private on 26.1, which is the only reason this entry is needed. Already public
# on 26.2 and 26.3, where widening it is a harmless no-op -- so the same entry
# works on all three and the common tree stays byte-identical across targets
# (PORTING_PLAN.md 10.1).
#
# This is the only entry in this file that does anything on 26.1. An earlier
# revision widened the static register instead and its comment claimed register
# was "private static"; javap shows it is public:
#
#   public static <T extends BlockEntity> BlockEntityType<T> register(
#       String, BlockEntitySupplier<? extends T>, Block...);
#
# That entry was therefore a no-op, and it was also the entry that made 26.2
# unbuildable, because Loom's validateAccessWidener rejects an entry naming a
# member that does not exist.
accessible method net/minecraft/world/level/block/entity/BlockEntityType <init> (Lnet/minecraft/world/level/block/entity/BlockEntityType$BlockEntitySupplier;Ljava/util/Set;)V

# Verified public on 26.1 and on 26.2, so on those two targets this entry does
# nothing. Retained because 1.21.11 declares it package-private -- its
# accessWidener has always carried this entry -- and because 26.3 has not been
# javap'd yet. A redundant "accessible" on a public member is a no-op, so keeping
# it costs nothing and saves a build if 26.3 tightened it.
accessible class net/minecraft/world/level/block/entity/BlockEntityType$BlockEntitySupplier

# --- Phase 2: creative tabs -----------------------------------------------
#
# CreativeModeTab.displayItems takes a DisplayItemsGenerator whose method takes a
# CreativeModeTab.Output parameter, so the parameter type has to be nameable
# even by a lambda that never mentions it. Verified public on 26.1 and on 26.2,
# so the entry is a no-op on both; kept for the same reason as the entry above.
accessible class net/minecraft/world/item/CreativeModeTab$Output

# --- Phase 4: menu types ---------------------------------------------------
#
# The previous revision of this block widened MenuType.register and claimed, in
# three separate ways, that it had no choice. javap -p on all four targets
# contradicts every part of that:
#
#   public net.minecraft.world.inventory.MenuType(
#       net.minecraft.world.inventory.MenuType$MenuSupplier<T>,
#       net.minecraft.world.flag.FeatureFlagSet);
#
#   public interface net.minecraft.world.inventory.MenuType$MenuSupplier<T ...> {
#       T create(int, Inventory);
#   }
#
# So the constructor is public and so is MenuSupplier. The block had mixed up the
# public 2-argument register with the private 3-argument varargs overload that
# sits beside it:
#
#   private static <T ...> MenuType<T> register(String, MenuSupplier<T>, FeatureFlag...);
#
# which is the one that is private, and which is also the reason the "deliberate"
# claim about FeatureFlagSet was wrong -- the public 2-arg register passes
# FeatureFlags.VANILLA_SET, quoted from its bytecode:
#
#   new MenuType
#   dup
#   aload_1
#   getstatic  FeatureFlags.VANILLA_SET
#   invokespecial MenuType.<init>(MenuSupplier, FeatureFlagSet)
#   invokestatic Registry.register(BuiltInRegistries.MENU, name, menuType)
#
# That last line is the bug the first runClient died on. MenuType.register is not
# a constructor, it registers as well -- under the String overload, so through
# withDefaultNamespace, i.e. as minecraft:workbench_menu. Decor4Fabric then handed
# the same instance to Registry.register under the correct decor4fabric key and
# got "Adding duplicate value ... to registry" during mod init.
#
# Hence this entry is on the constructor, not on register: Decor4Fabric builds the
# type with "new MenuType<>(supplier, FeatureFlags.VANILLA_SET)" and lets
# VanillaRegistrar.menuType perform the single registry write. Widening <init>
# rather than relying on javap's "public" is belt-and-braces, for the same reason
# BlockEntityType <init> is widened above: Prism's classtweaker->access-transformer
# conversion emits a bare "public net.minecraft.world.inventory.MenuType" entry for
# every nested type it widens, and javac then rejects the constructor as private
# until <init> is named explicitly.
accessible class net/minecraft/world/inventory/MenuType$MenuSupplier
accessible method net/minecraft/world/inventory/MenuType <init> (Lnet/minecraft/world/inventory/MenuType$MenuSupplier;Lnet/minecraft/world/flag/FeatureFlagSet;)V

# --- Phase 4: screen registration -------------------------------------------
#
# 26.x moved screen registration into vanilla: net.minecraft.client.gui.screens
# .MenuScreens holds the MenuType -> ScreenConstructor map and register is what
# writes to it. Before 26.x there was no such class in vanilla at all -- the 1.21.11
# jar has no MenuScreens entry -- so screen registration was a per-loader seam.
# Widening the vanilla method collapses that seam: the same call compiles on Fabric
# and on NeoForge, which is why WorkBenchScreen registration is not split by loader.
#
# register is private and ScreenConstructor is package-private, so -- as with
# MenuType above -- it takes two entries: the method to reach, the interface to
# name. Verified private/package-private with javap -p on all three 26.x targets,
# with an identical descriptor on each:
#
#   private static <M extends AbstractContainerMenu,
#                   U extends Screen & MenuAccess<M>>
#       void register(MenuType<? extends M>, ScreenConstructor<M, U>);
#     descriptor: (Lnet/minecraft/world/inventory/MenuType;
#                  Lnet/minecraft/client/gui/screens/MenuScreens$ScreenConstructor;)V
#
#   interface MenuScreens$ScreenConstructor<T extends AbstractContainerMenu,
#       U extends Screen & MenuAccess<T>>
#
# 1.21.11 needs no entry here, because it has nothing to widen; its equivalent is
# Fabric's ScreenRegistry and NeoForge's own MenuScreens, both in their own
# registries. That asymmetry is why the 1.21.11 accesswidener and this file are
# allowed to differ in more than their first line -- PORTING_PLAN.md 10.1 compares
# 26.1/26.2/26.3 against each other, not against 1.21.11.
accessible class net/minecraft/client/gui/screens/MenuScreens$ScreenConstructor
accessible method net/minecraft/client/gui/screens/MenuScreens register (Lnet/minecraft/world/inventory/MenuType;Lnet/minecraft/client/gui/screens/MenuScreens$ScreenConstructor;)V
